/-
Atiyah–Macdonald, *Introduction to Commutative Algebra* (Addison–Wesley, 1969),
**Exercise 9.3** (Chapter 9, "Discrete valuation rings and Dedekind domains").

## The statement formalised here

A valuation ring is Noetherian if and only if it is a discrete valuation ring.
A&M define a discrete valuation ring to be a *principal ideal domain with exactly one
non-zero prime ideal*; so a field is not a DVR.  Since a field is a Noetherian valuation
ring, the equivalence has to be stated either with the hypothesis that the ring is not a
field, or with the field alternative made explicit.  Both forms are proved below:

* `solution` : for a valuation ring `R`,
  `IsNoetherianRing R ↔ IsDiscreteValuationRing R ∨ IsField R`;
* `solution_nonfield` : for a valuation ring `R` with `¬ IsField R`,
  `IsNoetherianRing R ↔ IsDiscreteValuationRing R`  (the form matching the source proof);
* `solution_am` : the same, phrased with A&M's own definition of a DVR
  ("PID with exactly one non-zero prime ideal") instead of Mathlib's
  ("local PID which is not a field"); the two definitions are equivalent
  (`IsDiscreteValuationRing.iff_pid_with_one_nonzero_prime`).
* `valuationRing_iff_of_not_isField` : the equivalent "Noetherian domain" phrasing,
  `ValuationRing R ↔ IsDiscreteValuationRing R` under `[IsNoetherianRing R]` and
  `¬ IsField R`;
* `isDiscreteValuationRing_iff` : `IsDiscreteValuationRing R ↔
  ValuationRing R ∧ IsNoetherianRing R ∧ ¬ IsField R` (both directions at once).

## Proof, following the source natural-language solution

(⇒) In a valuation ring the principal ideals are linearly ordered: for nonzero `a b`
either `a ∣ b` or `b ∣ a` (`dvd_or_dvd`).  Hence `(a, b) = (a)` or `(b)`, and more
generally every finitely generated ideal is generated by one of its generators
(`span_pair_isPrincipal`, `isPrincipal_of_fg`).  If `R` is Noetherian every ideal is
finitely generated, so every ideal is principal and `R` is a PID
(`isPrincipalIdealRing_of_isNoetherianRing`).  A valuation ring is local; if it is not a
field its maximal ideal is nonzero, and since a nonzero prime ideal of a PID is maximal,
the maximal ideal is its only nonzero prime ideal (`existsUnique_nonzero_prime`).  By the
characterisation of DVRs as PIDs with exactly one nonzero prime ideal — this is the part
of A&M's Proposition 9.2 that is used as a *definition* in the book — `R` is a DVR
(`isDiscreteValuationRing_of_isPrincipalIdealRing`).

(⇐) In a DVR every nonzero ideal contains an element of least valuation, and every other
element of the ideal is divisible by it, so the ideal is principal; thus the ring is
Noetherian.  Formally: a DVR is a PID, hence Noetherian
(`isNoetherianRing_of_isDiscreteValuationRing`); the least-valuation element is
`exists_dvd_all_of_ne_bot`, and the sharper statement that every nonzero ideal is a power
of the maximal ideal is `exists_maximalIdeal_pow_eq`.

## Source
natural-language proof (public): https://j346huan.github.io/DoYourExercise/#/problem/AtiyahMcdonald69-9-3
(sourced from j346huan/DoYourExercise commit 80e1ebef1d7621c66aabb3920d981ffd8995ba91,
`.scratch/DoYourExercise/content/AtiyahMcdonald69/AtiyahMcdonald69-9-3/proof.tex`).

## Scope / implementation notes
* Everything is in Mathlib's language: a *valuation ring* is `ValuationRing R`
  (`IsDomain R` plus `a ∣ b ∨ b ∣ a`), and A&M's DVR definition is related to Mathlib's
  by `IsDiscreteValuationRing.iff_pid_with_one_nonzero_prime`.
* Standard facts already available in Mathlib are cited by name, not reproved:
  `ValuationRing.dvd_total` (principal ideals of a valuation ring are linearly ordered)
  and `IsNoetherian.noetherian` (ideals of a Noetherian ring are finitely generated).
  Everything else — f.g. ideals of a valuation ring are principal, Noetherian ⟹ PID,
  a non-field local PID is a DVR, and the converse implications — is proved here.
* No `sorry`, no axioms; see the `#print axioms` checks at the end of the file.
-/

import Mathlib.RingTheory.DiscreteValuationRing.TFAE
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Bezout

namespace AtiyahMcdonald69.Ex_9_3

open Ideal

/-! ### Valuation rings: divisibility is total and finitely generated ideals are principal -/

section ValuationRing

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]

/-- In a valuation ring the principal ideals are linearly ordered: for `a b : R` either
`a ∣ b` or `b ∣ a`.  (First line of the source proof.) -/
theorem dvd_or_dvd [ValuationRing R] (a b : R) : a ∣ b ∨ b ∣ a :=
  ValuationRing.dvd_total a b

/-- The ideal generated by two elements of a valuation ring is generated by one of the
two elements.  (Source proof: "every finitely generated ideal is generated by one of its
generators".) -/
theorem span_pair_isPrincipal [ValuationRing R] (x y : R) :
    (Ideal.span {x, y} : Ideal R).IsPrincipal := by
  rcases dvd_or_dvd x y with h | h
  · exact ⟨⟨x, span_pair_eq_span_left_iff_dvd.mpr h⟩⟩
  · exact ⟨⟨y, span_pair_eq_span_right_iff_dvd.mpr h⟩⟩

/-- Every finitely generated ideal of a valuation ring is principal.  The proof is the
textbook induction: an ideal of the form `(a₁) + ⋯ + (aₙ)` adds one generator at a time,
and `(x) + (y)` is `(x)` or `(y)`. -/
theorem isPrincipal_of_fg [ValuationRing R] {I : Ideal R} (hI : I.FG) : I.IsPrincipal := by
  refine Submodule.fg_induction (motive := fun N _ => N.IsPrincipal) ?_ ?_ I hI
  · intro x
    exact ⟨⟨x, rfl⟩⟩
  · rintro N₁ N₂ _ _ ⟨⟨x, rfl⟩⟩ ⟨⟨y, rfl⟩⟩
    rw [← Submodule.span_insert]
    exact span_pair_isPrincipal x y

/-- A Noetherian valuation ring is a principal ideal ring. -/
theorem isPrincipalIdealRing_of_isNoetherianRing [ValuationRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] :
    IsPrincipalIdealRing R where
  principal I := isPrincipal_of_fg (IsNoetherian.noetherian I)

end ValuationRing

/-! ### From a local PID to a discrete valuation ring (A&M, Proposition 9.2) -/

section LocalPID

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]

/-- A nonzero prime ideal of a PID is maximal; in particular a local PID which is not a
field has dimension one.  (Source proof: "a local principal ideal domain which is not a
field has dimension one".) -/
theorem isMaximal_of_isPrime_of_ne_bot [IsPrincipalIdealRing R] {P : Ideal R}
    (hP : P.IsPrime) (h : P ≠ ⊥) : P.IsMaximal := by
  have := hP
  exact IsPrime.to_maximal_ideal h

/-- A local PID which is not a field has exactly one nonzero prime ideal, namely its
maximal ideal. -/
theorem existsUnique_nonzero_prime [IsPrincipalIdealRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    (h : ¬ IsField R) : ∃! P : Ideal R, P ≠ ⊥ ∧ P.IsPrime := by
  have hmax : (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R).IsMaximal := IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal.isMaximal R
  refine ⟨IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R, ⟨?_, hmax.isPrime⟩, ?_⟩
  · exact Ring.ne_bot_of_isMaximal_of_not_isField hmax h
  · rintro P ⟨hPbot, hPprime⟩
    exact IsLocalRing.eq_maximalIdeal (isMaximal_of_isPrime_of_ne_bot hPprime hPbot)

/-- A local PID which is not a field is a discrete valuation ring: it is a PID with
exactly one nonzero prime ideal.  (Source proof: "Proposition 9.2 makes it a discrete
valuation ring".) -/
theorem isDiscreteValuationRing_of_isPrincipalIdealRing [IsPrincipalIdealRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    (h : ¬ IsField R) : IsDiscreteValuationRing R :=
  (IsDiscreteValuationRing.iff_pid_with_one_nonzero_prime R).mpr
    ⟨‹IsPrincipalIdealRing R›, existsUnique_nonzero_prime h⟩

end LocalPID

section Field

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R]

/-- A field is a Noetherian ring: its only ideals are `⊥` and `⊤`, which are finitely
generated.  (Needed for the field alternative in `solution`: a field is a valuation ring
but not a discrete valuation ring in the sense of A&M.) -/
theorem isNoetherianRing_of_isField (h : IsField R) : IsNoetherianRing R := by
  rw [isNoetherianRing_iff_ideal_fg]
  intro I
  by_cases hI : I = ⊥
  · rw [hI]
    exact Submodule.fg_bot
  · obtain ⟨x, hxI, hx0⟩ := Submodule.exists_mem_ne_zero_of_ne_bot hI
    rw [Ideal.eq_top_of_isUnit_mem I hxI
      (isUnit_iff_exists_inv.mpr (IsField.mul_inv_cancel h hx0))]
    exact Ideal.fg_top R

end Field

/-! ### Exercise 9.3 -/

section Exercise

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]

/-- Exercise 9.3 (⇒), non-field form: a Noetherian valuation ring which is not a field
is a discrete valuation ring. -/
theorem isDiscreteValuationRing_of_isNoetherianRing [ValuationRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R]
    (h : ¬ IsField R) : IsDiscreteValuationRing R := by
  have := isPrincipalIdealRing_of_isNoetherianRing (R := R)
  exact isDiscreteValuationRing_of_isPrincipalIdealRing h

/-- Exercise 9.3 (⇐): a discrete valuation ring is Noetherian. -/
theorem isNoetherianRing_of_isDiscreteValuationRing [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] :
    IsNoetherianRing R :=
  PrincipalIdealRing.isNoetherianRing

/-- A discrete valuation ring is a valuation ring (a local PID has comparable principal
ideals). -/
theorem valuationRing_of_isDiscreteValuationRing [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] :
    ValuationRing R :=
  inferInstance

/-- Exercise 9.3 (⇐), the "least valuation" argument of the source proof: in a discrete
valuation ring every nonzero ideal contains a nonzero element dividing all elements of
the ideal, hence the ideal is principal. -/
theorem exists_dvd_all_of_ne_bot [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] {I : Ideal R} (hI : I ≠ ⊥) :
    ∃ x ∈ I, x ≠ 0 ∧ ∀ y ∈ I, x ∣ y := by
  have : I.IsPrincipal := IsPrincipalIdealRing.principal I
  refine ⟨Submodule.IsPrincipal.generator I, Submodule.IsPrincipal.generator_mem I, ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro h0
    exact hI ((Submodule.IsPrincipal.eq_bot_iff_generator_eq_zero I).mpr h0)
  · intro y hy
    exact (Submodule.IsPrincipal.mem_iff_generator_dvd I).mp hy

/-- Exercise 9.3 (⇐), sharp form: in a discrete valuation ring every nonzero ideal is a
power of the maximal ideal. -/
theorem exists_maximalIdeal_pow_eq [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] {I : Ideal R} (hI : I ≠ ⊥) :
    ∃ n : ℕ, I = IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R ^ n :=
  exists_maximalIdeal_pow_eq_of_principal R
    (IsPrincipalIdealRing.principal (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R)) I hI

/-- **Exercise 9.3**, form with the field case made explicit: a valuation ring is
Noetherian if and only if it is a discrete valuation ring or a field. -/
theorem solution [ValuationRing R] :
    IsNoetherianRing R ↔ IsDiscreteValuationRing R ∨ IsField R := by
  constructor
  · intro hNoeth
    by_cases hF : IsField R
    · exact Or.inr hF
    · exact Or.inl (@isDiscreteValuationRing_of_isNoetherianRing R _ _ _ hNoeth hF)
  · rintro (hDVR | hF)
    · have := hDVR
      exact isNoetherianRing_of_isDiscreteValuationRing
    · exact isNoetherianRing_of_isField hF

/-- **Exercise 9.3**, non-field form: a valuation ring which is not a field is Noetherian
if and only if it is a discrete valuation ring. -/
theorem solution_nonfield [ValuationRing R] (h : ¬ IsField R) :
    IsNoetherianRing R ↔ IsDiscreteValuationRing R := by
  constructor
  · intro hNoeth
    exact @isDiscreteValuationRing_of_isNoetherianRing R _ _ _ hNoeth h
  · intro hDVR
    exact @isNoetherianRing_of_isDiscreteValuationRing R _ _ hDVR

/-- **Exercise 9.3** with A&M's own definition of a discrete valuation ring, "a principal
ideal domain with exactly one nonzero prime ideal": a valuation ring is Noetherian iff it
is such a ring or a field. -/
theorem solution_am [ValuationRing R] :
    IsNoetherianRing R ↔
      (IsPrincipalIdealRing R ∧ ∃! P : Ideal R, P ≠ ⊥ ∧ P.IsPrime) ∨ IsField R := by
  rw [solution]
  constructor
  · rintro (hDVR | hF)
    · exact Or.inl ((IsDiscreteValuationRing.iff_pid_with_one_nonzero_prime R).mp hDVR)
    · exact Or.inr hF
  · rintro (⟨hPID, huniq⟩ | hF)
    · exact Or.inl ((IsDiscreteValuationRing.iff_pid_with_one_nonzero_prime R).mpr ⟨hPID, huniq⟩)
    · exact Or.inr hF

/-- Restatement with the Noetherian hypothesis fixed, as in the book: a Noetherian domain
is a valuation ring if and only if it is a discrete valuation ring.  (The field case is
excluded, as in the source proof: a field is a Noetherian valuation ring but not a DVR.) -/
theorem valuationRing_iff_of_not_isField [IsNoetherianRing R] (h : ¬ IsField R) :
    ValuationRing R ↔ IsDiscreteValuationRing R := by
  constructor
  · intro hval
    exact @isDiscreteValuationRing_of_isNoetherianRing R _ _ hval ‹IsNoetherianRing R› h
  · intro hDVR
    exact @valuationRing_of_isDiscreteValuationRing R _ _ hDVR

/-- A domain is a discrete valuation ring exactly when it is a Noetherian valuation ring
which is not a field (both directions of Exercise 9.3 together). -/
theorem isDiscreteValuationRing_iff :
    IsDiscreteValuationRing R ↔ ValuationRing R ∧ IsNoetherianRing R ∧ ¬ IsField R := by
  constructor
  · intro hDVR
    have := hDVR
    exact ⟨valuationRing_of_isDiscreteValuationRing,
      isNoetherianRing_of_isDiscreteValuationRing, IsDiscreteValuationRing.not_isField R⟩
  · rintro ⟨hval, hnoeth, hfield⟩
    have := hval
    exact isDiscreteValuationRing_of_isNoetherianRing hfield

end Exercise

end AtiyahMcdonald69.Ex_9_3

